WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:06.543
How could one cell tell a moving object from a moving world?

2
00:00:07.533 --> 00:00:13.293
In 2012, Zhang and colleagues recorded from W3 cells in isolated mouse retina.

3
00:00:14.333 --> 00:00:15.453
Start with a spot.

4
00:00:16.433 --> 00:00:18.533
Brighten a small spot over the cell, and it fires.

5
00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:21.987
Dim the same spot, and it fires again.

6
00:00:22.967 --> 00:00:27.907
The cell takes excitation from the ON pathway, driven when light goes up, and from

7
00:00:27.907 --> 00:00:28.907
the OFF pathway.

8
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:31.880
So a change of either sign excites it.

9
00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:34.380
The two inputs add rather than cancel.

10
00:00:35.433 --> 00:00:40.813
Now motion. A small textured patch moves over the cell's centre, and the background

11
00:00:40.813 --> 00:00:45.593
stays still. The distant surround is a wide ring far outside the centre.

12
00:00:46.617 --> 00:00:48.277
The cell fires many spikes.

13
00:00:49.097 --> 00:00:50.097
Say 40.

14
00:00:50.717 --> 00:00:52.537
Now the whole scene moves together.

15
00:00:53.417 --> 00:00:55.517
The centre gets exactly the same stimulus.

16
00:00:56.617 --> 00:01:01.317
Predict. If the cell listened only to its centre, what would it do now?

17
00:01:07.317 --> 00:01:08.917
It would fire just as much.

18
00:01:09.797 --> 00:01:12.457
It does not. It falls nearly silent.

19
00:01:13.397 --> 00:01:14.397
Say 3 spikes.

20
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.640
So motion far out in the surround must be silencing it.

21
00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:23.180
Zhang and colleagues found that this happens in two places at once.

22
00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:25.580
Part is presynaptic.

23
00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:30.500
The excitation is suppressed before it reaches the cell, so less of it arrives.

24
00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:33.030
Part is postsynaptic.

25
00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:36.310
Inhibition lands on the W3 cell itself.

26
00:01:37.433 --> 00:01:43.473
Older work on similar cells in other species proposed wide-field amacrine cells as

27
00:01:43.473 --> 00:01:44.953
the source of this kind of suppression.

28
00:01:46.067 --> 00:01:47.967
Where does the excitation come from?

29
00:01:48.827 --> 00:01:51.307
Much of it does not come straight from bipolar cells.

30
00:01:52.267 --> 00:01:56.947
It arrives through the VG3 amacrine cell, an excitatory amacrine cell.

31
00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:02.520
So the circuit that builds the selection starts before the ganglion cell sees the

32
00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:03.520
signal.

33
00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:05.870
Two amacrine cells, two jobs.

34
00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:08.730
VG3 brings excitation in.

35
00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:11.410
Wide-field cells are the proposed brake.

36
00:02:12.383 --> 00:02:14.883
A W3 cell answers one question.

37
00:02:15.763 --> 00:02:18.223
Is something small moving differently from everything else?

38
00:02:19.217 --> 00:02:22.477
When the eye or head turns, the whole image slides together.

39
00:02:23.517 --> 00:02:27.917
The authors proposed these cells as alarm neurons for overhead predators.

40
00:02:29.267 --> 00:02:30.547
That is a hypothesis.

41
00:02:31.607 --> 00:02:36.567
The experiments used isolated retina, and no behavioural test of necessity was done.

42
00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:40.430
A second channel is the JAM-B cell, an OFF cell.

43
00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:44.330
Most labelled cells had a lopsided dendritic arbor.

44
00:02:45.283 --> 00:02:49.783
These lopsided cells preferred movement from the cell body toward the dendrites.

45
00:02:52.417 --> 00:02:54.577
The filter is built inside the retina.

46
00:02:55.457 --> 00:02:58.517
The scene motion this cell ignores still travels in other channels.
